WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:02.598
I exchange my call sign with someone in Slovakia.

00:00:02.598 --> 00:00:07.300
If the conditions are just right--and that depends on weather and sun spot activity and stuff--

00:00:07.450 --> 00:00:10.600
You can really get your RF out across the world.

00:00:10.700 --> 00:00:16.800
2 meter band is generally considered to be much closer range.

00:00:16.990 --> 00:00:20.222
(Are we looking at the post-apocalyptic internet here?)

00:00:20.222 --> 00:00:21.539
I think so, yeah.

00:00:21.539 --> 00:00:24.800
(So, after civilization falls apart...)

00:00:24.939 --> 00:00:29.220
I'll be talking to Dave over... making sure everything's OK.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:32.900
It seemed to die--briefly.

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:36.779
I'm scared to touch anything because it will suddenly die

00:00:36.940 --> 00:00:39.065
(So this is quite flaky stuff, right?)

00:00:39.065 --> 00:00:41.250
It mainly-- it's normally very reliable

00:00:41.250 --> 00:00:44.099
the problem is that we have these cables which I made myself and, ah...

00:00:45.040 --> 00:00:47.040
Yeah, don't look at this one.

00:00:49.300 --> 00:00:53.200
(Why have we got two ThinkPads sitting on the desk in front of us?)

00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:54.820
...going to sleep... Well,

00:00:54.820 --> 00:01:02.369
I thought it might be interesting to talk a little bit about packet radio and maybe touch on amateur radio in general as well.

00:01:02.859 --> 00:01:04.859
Packet radio is...

00:01:05.619 --> 00:01:10.900
It's essentially sending data over the radio waves and

00:01:10.900 --> 00:01:15.740
the frequency spectrum I'm using at the moment is the amateur radio bands.

00:01:15.740 --> 00:01:22.160
So this is 2-meter band, what I'm using now, and that's about 144-145 MHz

00:01:22.630 --> 00:01:25.979
2-meter band is probably the band with the most packet radio

00:01:26.920 --> 00:01:31.900
Because there's something called "APRS" which is the Automatic Packet Reporting System

00:01:31.900 --> 00:01:34.589
That's all based upon one protocol called "AX.25"

00:01:36.040 --> 00:01:38.040
and this is kind of a...

00:01:38.650 --> 00:01:40.140
Sort of... It's AX.25, but...

00:01:40.140 --> 00:01:49.800
modified to accommodate some of the things required to make it work on amateur radio, instead of over some kind of cable.

00:01:49.800 --> 00:01:54.209
Okay. So we've got two of the cheapest ham radios. These are Baofeng UV-5Rs

00:01:54.610 --> 00:01:58.409
We've got two laptops, and we've got two-- what are essentially modems,

00:01:58.409 --> 00:02:03.059
they're called, "terminal node controllers," and they essentially take data through a serial input and

00:02:03.460 --> 00:02:07.760
turn it into some some sort of strange sound, a bit like dial-up

00:02:09.480 --> 00:02:14.260
Essentially, we're encapsulating what we would normally put on Ethernet, but

00:02:14.420 --> 00:02:20.380
We're encapsulating into what's called "KISS framing," and on top of that: AX.25

00:02:20.420 --> 00:02:23.359
I think I should probably draw this out, because going it's a bit complicated...

00:02:24.240 --> 00:02:30.049
Let's draw a radio--and then we have our modems, or terminal node controllers, and

00:02:30.240 --> 00:02:33.499
these are talking to the radio. This is actually an audio cable,

00:02:33.500 --> 00:02:38.180
So this is just audio going straight into the radio. And then we have two computers,

00:02:39.420 --> 00:02:41.420
...this is my laptop...

00:02:42.140 --> 00:02:46.560
And this is a serial cable. I will draw the frame, the TCP/IP...

00:02:46.640 --> 00:02:51.840
At the very bottom we have KISS which stands for "keep it simple stupid," and

00:02:52.020 --> 00:02:55.400
the way this type of framing works is that if you send a zero,

00:02:55.440 --> 00:02:58.460
that means there's a data frame coming in. So it's very simple.

00:02:59.240 --> 00:03:01.660
After that we have

00:03:01.700 --> 00:03:06.260
AX.25, and this essentially talks at hardware addresses

00:03:06.260 --> 00:03:13.160
So what you might typically consider to be MAC addresses, but in the case of radio--it's actually my callsign, which is-- M6PIU

00:03:14.460 --> 00:03:17.300
And... (And if you need to get in touch with Aaron...)

00:03:17.340 --> 00:03:18.990
Get on the radio (...urgently!)

00:03:18.990 --> 00:03:26.930
Yeah, and then so this AX.25 actually becomes the lowest layer, in the setup that I've got here, over the TCP/IP stack.

00:03:26.930 --> 00:03:30.799
So this is, let's say M6PIU,

00:03:31.320 --> 00:03:35.200
to my friend, G1ZEK.

00:03:35.200 --> 00:03:41.000
This guy actually gave me this TNC here, so

00:03:41.100 --> 00:03:45.080
This is very low-level framing of messages.

00:03:45.080 --> 00:03:49.460
It's just saying, "pass this data over to this, and this is the the length of the data."

00:03:49.520 --> 00:03:55.160
This entire thing is going to be the the TCP/IP stack, with this framing underneath it.

00:03:55.320 --> 00:03:57.600
On top of that, we have the internet layer.

00:03:57.740 --> 00:04:02.260
So, this internet layer works on the basis of IP addresses, so

00:04:02.880 --> 00:04:05.960
Ham radio operators are fortunate enough to be allocated:

00:04:06.300 --> 00:04:11.600
44.0.0.0/8

00:04:11.600 --> 00:04:14.460
That's a huge range of IP addresses, and

00:04:14.460 --> 00:04:18.460
You can, as a ham radio operator, be allocated a small portion of that.

00:04:18.470 --> 00:04:22.999
This is the kind of level that the Internet Protocol layer runs at

00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:28.190
Actual IP addresses rather than Ethernet addresses or call signs as in the case of AX.25.

00:04:28.590 --> 00:04:30.829
If you look in arp.c in the Linux kernel,

00:04:30.829 --> 00:04:36.340
you'll see specific references to AX.25, for the way that it formats callsigns, as if they were

00:04:36.560 --> 00:04:39.160
Ethernet addresses, MAC addresses.

00:04:39.599 --> 00:04:45.229
On top of the internet layer, we have the transport layer. This is TCP... so this is TCP over IP...

00:04:46.080 --> 00:04:48.080
Let's just call it TCP

00:04:48.320 --> 00:04:50.940
It could also be UDP

00:04:51.020 --> 00:04:54.340
And on top of this, we have the application.

00:04:54.480 --> 00:04:58.700
Maybe we can talk about what's actually being transmitted right now:

00:04:58.700 --> 00:05:02.580
Every five seconds, we're sending a ping. It's at the same level of TCP/UDP

00:05:02.580 --> 00:05:09.770
so, we could also write "ping" here, and you'd also have--for example, "ARP"--here. This is the address resolution protocol.

00:05:09.770 --> 00:05:15.080
It's converting Internet addresses into Ethernet or call signs.

00:05:15.120 --> 00:05:20.400
When I want to send a ping to this machine, essentially what's happening is,

00:05:20.400 --> 00:05:27.860
my computer first uses ARP to resolve the call sign for the IP address. So it says,

00:05:27.880 --> 00:05:35.520
"who is 44.131.56.17 tell [...]"

00:05:35.520 --> 00:05:39.680
and then the address that it's coming from. So in this case,

00:05:39.800 --> 00:05:44.480
44.131.56.16

00:05:44.680 --> 00:05:48.280
So-- 17, if it's available,

00:05:48.540 --> 00:05:53.280
will respond to this packet and say that,

00:05:53.280 --> 00:06:03.200
44.131.56.17 is at M6PIU-[some SSID]

00:06:03.260 --> 00:06:06.760
So in the case, I think one of these is 0 and the other one is 1.

00:06:06.940 --> 00:06:13.140
(Is that different devices?) Yes, because the call sign is being used as the Ethernet address in a typical sense,

00:06:14.700 --> 00:06:19.840
You can't have the same address in two places, especially if you use more than two machines, because

00:06:19.840 --> 00:06:27.600
they would both respond, or, they would both end up receiving a packet destined to a certain IP address

00:06:29.880 --> 00:06:34.409
Once you know the call sign of the Ethernet address,

00:06:34.980 --> 00:06:42.420
the packets can be actually sent via AX.25, because that's the the only level above AX.25

00:06:42.420 --> 00:06:47.100
It doesn't understand anything past that, it's just call signs and packet length.

00:06:47.180 --> 00:06:50.020
This information is going to be cached for a while.

00:06:50.020 --> 00:06:53.020
I forget how long exactly, maybe five or ten minutes?

00:06:53.340 --> 00:06:58.080
Or until it fails to contact the remote host again.

00:06:58.080 --> 00:07:02.669
(I've got a vision of the airwaves being full, with lots of this happening from lots of devices,

00:07:02.669 --> 00:07:04.709
but on the same channel, is that how it works?)

00:07:05.160 --> 00:07:08.639
Lots of devices can use the same frequency with AX.25,

00:07:08.919 --> 00:07:14.429
because the packets are being sent with a sender and a recipient in each packet.

00:07:14.429 --> 00:07:16.429
(You get clashes, though?)

00:07:16.920 --> 00:07:22.060
You do-- actually one of the biggest problems with running modern-day Linux on

00:07:22.400 --> 00:07:28.960
Ham radio is that it's expecting the network hardware to respond very quickly

00:07:29.400 --> 00:07:36.380
So when it does this step, it says, "who has this address, tell whoever," it ha s already requested the same information again

00:07:36.420 --> 00:07:41.300
before it's even gone out of the the modem.

00:07:41.460 --> 00:07:46.320
So, you have to set this to a more reasonable time, like 5 seconds or something, instead of 100.

00:07:46.560 --> 00:07:49.479
If you're continuously requesting the same information,

00:07:49.480 --> 00:07:53.320
the recipient has no time to respond, because this is only half duplex,

00:07:53.320 --> 00:07:59.060
only one person on the radio wave, or the frequency, can speak at a time.

00:07:59.080 --> 00:08:00.990
This one is actually pinging right now.

00:08:00.990 --> 00:08:03.419
I've set the interval to five seconds and it takes about

00:08:03.600 --> 00:08:08.140
2.6 seconds for a ping to reach its destination and be replied to.

00:08:08.140 --> 00:08:15.720
What we have here is machine 0 pinging machine 1, so this is from--no ARP information cached at all yet--

00:08:15.720 --> 00:08:19.590
So, the first packet that we received is coming from M6PIU,

00:08:19.590 --> 00:08:26.220
with some SSID, going to QST, and QST is kind of USQ-code-speak for,

00:08:26.220 --> 00:08:28.400
"All operators that are listening,"

00:08:28.400 --> 00:08:31.000
so we're saying, to everyone that's listening,

00:08:31.000 --> 00:08:38.300
"Tell me who has this address, 41.131.56.17." This gets sent a couple of times, and eventually,

00:08:38.320 --> 00:08:43.220
machine 1 responds to machine 0, so it's coming again from me...

00:08:43.240 --> 00:08:44.560
...to me...

00:08:44.560 --> 00:08:47.760
...and it's saying that machine 1 address is

00:08:47.900 --> 00:08:54.280
44.131.56.17, and it's at this, sort of Ethernet address, or, hardware address

00:08:54.540 --> 00:09:00.340
This hardware address is actually my callsign, encoded in some strange way with some bit shifting,

00:09:00.340 --> 00:09:05.840
just to make the character set fit into what would typically be a hardware address.

00:09:05.860 --> 00:09:10.880
This happens a few times, because not all packets are sent successfully.

00:09:11.060 --> 00:09:15.320
Sometimes, it's quite impatient and will send the same packet again, especially in the beginning,

00:09:15.320 --> 00:09:20.680
but once machine 0 has learned that the hardware address, my callsign,

00:09:20.680 --> 00:09:25.640
is associated with the IP address of Machine 1, it can start sending ping requests.

00:09:25.880 --> 00:09:30.320
These purple packets are ICMP, which is the pinging protocol.

00:09:30.320 --> 00:09:33.780
Here we have two ping requests, and then two responses.

00:09:33.789 --> 00:09:40.140
These are going at a higher layer now in the TCP/IP stack. They're using IP addresses as opposed to hardware addresses.

00:09:40.700 --> 00:09:46.780
What we ended up with is a ping response time of about two and a half seconds,

00:09:46.780 --> 00:09:52.600
which is not bad -- considering we're running at, probably around, 1.2 kilobits per second.

00:09:52.600 --> 00:09:59.140
(This is a closed demo, with two machines and two radios, does anyone else have that?)

00:09:59.140 --> 00:10:04.660
I quite frequently talk to my friend G1ZEK over AX.25

00:10:04.720 --> 00:10:07.120
Not via TCP, but at a lower level.

00:10:07.120 --> 00:10:14.120
If you don't connect up the stuff, or if you don't activate the TCP layers above this,

00:10:14.120 --> 00:10:19.780
you can connect to these just by a serial terminal, such as GNU Screen,

00:10:19.780 --> 00:10:22.680
and you can exchange messages, just as text.

00:10:22.740 --> 00:10:29.140
It's much more reliable than trying to get TCP/IP working off of all of this. (So, because you've got these layers working on TCP/IP...)

00:10:29.340 --> 00:10:31.940
(what does that give us that you wouldn't get another way?)

00:10:31.940 --> 00:10:36.540
(Would you be able to, say, send a website or...?) We can definitely send a website.

00:10:36.540 --> 00:10:39.700
I can show you that, it will take quite a while to load.

00:10:39.980 --> 00:10:45.320
It will be a very small website. Let's set up a small web server, and

00:10:45.580 --> 00:10:48.840
try to load that page. One of the problems is that--

00:10:49.370 --> 00:10:56.620
So really, operating two radios in such close proximity is a terrible idea. You can actually damage them. I've got these set to 1 watt,

00:10:57.340 --> 00:11:01.340
and I tried to put as much space between them as I can.

00:11:02.840 --> 00:11:06.440
The problem is that with these very cheap

00:11:06.460 --> 00:11:11.940
USB-to-serial adapters, they can crash if there's too much RF around, so

00:11:12.200 --> 00:11:16.140
a lot of the time I have to keep on restarting this, because

00:11:16.240 --> 00:11:18.560
It breaks basically.

00:11:18.900 --> 00:11:22.720
Let's try and load a page.

00:11:22.720 --> 00:11:25.140
(so the flashing there is connection... the packets being sent...)

00:11:25.160 --> 00:11:26.290
Yeah, so on this side

00:11:26.290 --> 00:11:31.540
It says X method for transmit and receive. You can see that when transmit is red here,

00:11:31.620 --> 00:11:35.700
receiver's red over on this side, so they should be alternating.

00:11:35.700 --> 00:11:38.360
Ping actually takes up a significant amount of bandwidth, when...

00:11:38.360 --> 00:11:45.819
here at this speed, so I'll kill ping. It seems to be working. So your web browser open. I propose a little page earlier

00:11:45.819 --> 00:11:47.819
it's only one sentence, but...

00:11:48.649 --> 00:11:51.518
(What we lookin' at for a sentence? A minute? Several minutes?)

00:11:51.620 --> 00:11:56.199
It depends on how reliable it is, because it could take hours at this rate.

00:11:56.960 --> 00:12:02.980
But a single sentence, I don't know maybe if you have everything tuned quite precisely maybe

00:12:03.640 --> 00:12:09.240
20 seconds or something. (Shall I ask a silly question: pictures?) I have done it.

00:12:10.120 --> 00:12:17.840
It took maybe 10 minutes, but a picture of me has transmi... Well, probably it's out of space now, you know a

00:12:19.819 --> 00:12:21.639
A very weak signal of my face.

00:12:21.639 --> 00:12:28.448
This machine on the Left received a packet saying, "here is this address, tell that one..." but, it doesn't seem to have responded...

00:12:28.720 --> 00:12:32.120
These connectors are also very flaky...

00:12:32.680 --> 00:12:34.680
Give them a squeeze...

00:12:36.760 --> 00:12:38.760
Come on, you can do it!

00:12:40.380 --> 00:12:47.120
From the activity light here, it looks like pings are being sent, and there are responses coming through,

00:12:47.220 --> 00:12:51.300
But currently, it says that destination host is unreachable.

00:12:51.520 --> 00:12:55.300
What you can do, instead of using these things, is actually use the sound card and

00:12:56.160 --> 00:13:00.840
that's sort of become a lot more popular now, I quite like these because

00:13:00.840 --> 00:13:03.280
(Well you can see a lot more information there...)

00:13:03.280 --> 00:13:08.880
Yeah, I think they're much more cool than using a sound card. It has timed out, what a surprise.

00:13:08.880 --> 00:13:11.100
(...why [unintelligible] data...)

00:13:11.220 --> 00:13:17.100
It's showing the packets being exchanged, so they're definitely talking to each other right now.

00:13:17.100 --> 00:13:21.160
There's a lot of packets which are becoming out of order or being re-transmitted.

00:13:21.160 --> 00:13:23.490
Yeah, I think it has timed-out again.

00:13:23.490 --> 00:13:31.169
Let's try using Curl. Curl is just simple command-line utility to request web pages and files from web servers.

00:13:31.540 --> 00:13:34.820
The Python web server here picked up that I requested a page.

00:13:35.260 --> 00:13:37.740
(Okay, that's a first start, right?)

00:13:37.740 --> 00:13:42.800
Yeah, it hasn't-- AH! The page  has loaded! But only in Curl--I don't want to show you...

00:13:42.800 --> 00:13:49.360
...but I will, it's just down here. So this is the HTML that I typed in, which is this "hello computer" file from M6PIU.

00:13:49.360 --> 00:13:54.080
So we're going to try and load it in the web browser now. (It's all your CSS, right?)

00:13:54.600 --> 00:13:59.180
Oh yeah, for this website, and on my personal one, it's loaded with CSS.

00:14:00.020 --> 00:14:02.020
[ laughter ]

00:14:02.560 --> 00:14:08.040
Should have timed it, shouldn't we? Just to be sure, opening a new tab

00:14:09.420 --> 00:14:10.500
(a lot of re-sent...)

00:14:10.840 --> 00:14:13.560
Yes, I just saw her on this little Python web server that

00:14:14.079 --> 00:14:21.568
The page was requested. Also, I got it working this morning, it was fine. So these are our... A-HA! It's done!

00:14:22.569 --> 00:14:25.709
There you go. "Hello, computerphile," I spelled it right, I think...

00:14:26.340 --> 00:14:31.520
I think, in the early days, it probably contributed somewhat to research

00:14:31.520 --> 00:14:39.420
towards TCP/IP and other network protocols, especially on slow connections, unreliable connections, like this.

00:14:41.380 --> 00:14:50.000
And I like to think of it as the grandfather of 
GPRS and Wi-Fi and everything that works now.

00:14:50.510 --> 00:14:56.169
You would have to s-boxes here to 4-bit s boxes for example plugging into one 8-bit permutation box

00:14:56.240 --> 00:15:01.510
So the outputs of these get jumbled about a mixed up here the way an SP network works is it?

00:15:01.670 --> 00:15:09.130
looks a lot more rounded you have some of questions and things actually these numbers were chosen 3/16 5/16 or 1/16 7/16

